     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-10-05 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Congo Ebola Death Toll Exceeds 4,000 Amid Center Attacks and Worker Strikes</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Ebola</span> <span>#Congo</span> <span>#Health Crisis</span> <span>#Humanitarian</span></div>
       <p>The Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) has recorded over 4,000 deaths in its ongoing Ebola outbreak, with the Health Ministry reporting 4,018 fatalities as of Thursday, October 1, 2026. These deaths account for nearly half of the 8,300 confirmed cases, making it the fastest-growing Ebola outbreak in history. The outbreak, announced on May 15, is caused by the rare Bundibugyo virus, for which no known treatment or vaccine exists.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-05/congo-ebola-death-toll-exceeds-4000-amid-center-attacks-and-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Efforts to contain the virus are severely hampered by insecurity and community resistance. Julien Harneis, the UN senior Ebola coordinator, noted that creating Ebola transit and treatment centres has been a significant struggle, with money and staff in short supply. This challenge was exacerbated when an Ebola-hit camp housing a transit centre in Kigonze, on the outskirts of Bunia, Ituri province, was burned down by soldiers searching for weapons earlier this week. The incident forced 19,000 people to flee, with the United Nations stating, “The enormous challenge of halting the spread of Ebola in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) just became even harder.” Harneis added that losing a centre means losing capacity and investment, hindering access to critical care.</p><p>The crisis is further complicated by widespread mistrust and worker unrest. Health workers in Bunia, the capital of northeastern Ituri province, protested for unpaid wages, carrying placards that read “No money, no data!” and “No payment for months.” Front-line workers have gone on strike multiple times since the outbreak began, disrupting containment efforts. An aid worker with Médecins Sans Frontieres (Doctors Without Borders) contracted Ebola while in Congo and was evacuated to a high-level isolation unit in the Netherlands, highlighting the risks faced by responders. MSF described the human cost as “unprecedented,” with over 50 health workers having died from the virus.</p><p>Moreover, political tensions surrounding the outbreak have turned violent. A Congolese politician, Marie-Célestin Karondwa, a local official with the ruling Union for Democracy and Social Progress party, was attacked and beaten to death at his home in Butembo, an Ebola hot spot in eastern North Kivu province, for defending government measures. His house was subsequently set on fire. The World Health Organisation has raised concerns about the virus's rapid spread into new health zones, though Africa CDC observed a recent fall in new confirmed cases, attributing it to insecurity and challenges in community cooperation. Despite a slowdown in some areas, the spread has accelerated in others, creating a delicate situation as the Central African nation grapples with its 17th Ebola outbreak amid deep public distrust stemming from past outbreaks and corruption.</p><p>What to watch: Efforts to rebuild treatment capacity and address community mistrust.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thoroughly covers the complex Ebola outbreak update, integrating figures, quotes, and specific incidents cleanly.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/ebola-treatment-centre-was-burned-down-as-death-toll-in-congo-passes-4000/article71537063.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
