     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-10-05 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>FDA Approves Autus Adjustable Heart Valve for Pediatric Patients</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Medical Technology</span> <span>#FDA Approval</span> <span>#Pediatrics</span></div>
       <p>The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has granted approval for the Autus Size-Adjustable Valve, developed by Edwards, marking the first artificial heart valve designed to expand post-surgery and grow with children. This innovative pulmonary valve, intended for pediatric patients, aims to eliminate the need for multiple surgical procedures as a child develops.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-05/fda-approves-autus-adjustable-heart-valve-for-pediatric-pati.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Congenital heart disease affects approximately 400,000 children born in the U.S. each year, according to a study published in the National Library of Medicine. About 30 percent of these cases involve heart valves, which regulate blood flow. Specifically, abnormalities of the pulmonary valve, such as stenosis (a narrowed valve) and atresia (a valve failing to form normally), impact around 4,200 babies annually in the United States, as reported by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention.</p><p>The Autus valve can be implanted with a diameter as small as 13 millimeters in toddlers and preschool-aged children. It can also be pre-expanded for older patients before surgery. Its key advancement is the ability to be expanded inside the body using a balloon catheter, allowing it to keep pace with a child's growth. This adjustability enables the valve to reach a maximum of 22 millimeters in diameter, a size comparable to artificial valves used in adults, thereby significantly reducing surgical risks.</p><p>Beyond its adjustable nature, the Autus valve is also notable for being the first to utilize polymeric material for its flaps. Traditional implants often feature synthetic flaps derived from animal materials, which tend to harden and degrade more quickly in children, necessitating further surgeries. The FDA indicates that one-year follow-up data for the Autus suggests superior durability compared to existing models, offering a promising long-term solution for pediatric cardiac care.</p><p>What to watch: Future studies on long-term durability and broader patient impact.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately summarizes the source, including the medical conditions and the technical advancements of the Autus valve.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2275489/fda-approves-autus-size-adjustable-artificial-heart-valve-for-kids/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-10-05/fda-approves-autus-adjustable-heart-valve-for-pediatric-pati" data-title="FDA Approves Autus Adjustable Heart Valve for Pediatric Patients"><b>Share:</b>
