     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-05 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Nepal Avalanche Study Highlights Alpine Risks for New Zealand</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>University of Canterbury researcher Dr. Robinson joined an international team investigating a massive August 26 Himalayan rock and ice avalanche. The study concluded that human-induced climate change, which has raised local temperatures by up to 1.9°C, combined with lingering damage from a 2015 earthquake to destabilize the mountain terrain.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-05/nepal-avalanche-study-highlights-alpine-risks-for-new-zealan.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Robinson warns that Aotearoa New Zealand faces similar risks due to warming temperatures and receding glaciers. While Southern Alps events are expected to be smaller than the Nepal disaster, rising tourism in areas like Aoraki Mount Cook National Park increases the potential for human exposure to these complex, climate-driven geological hazards.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is accurate, concise, and correctly attributes the research findings and warnings to Dr. Robinson.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-nepal-disaster-highlights-nz-mountains.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
