     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-10-05 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tesla Rolls Out Emergency Drive Away Feature After Supercharger Shooting</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tesla</span> <span>#Supercharger</span> <span>#Safety Feature</span> <span>#Emergency</span></div>
       <p>Tesla has introduced a new safety feature called Emergency Drive Away, enabling drivers to move their vehicles even if the Supercharger is still connected, specifically for emergency situations. This development comes a couple of months after a driver was shot inside a Tesla taxi while charging at an In-N-Out Burger location in Twin Falls, Idaho.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-05/tesla-rolls-out-emergency-drive-away-feature-after-superchar.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Under normal circumstances, to shift from Park to Drive after charging, a Tesla owner must unlock the charge port using the vehicle's infotainment system, the mobile app, or by pressing a button on the charger handle. This procedure can be a critical impediment when a rapid departure is necessary. Tesla owners had called for a quick escape system following the shooting incident, prompting the automaker's response.</p><p>The company announced Emergency Drive Away on X, providing a short demonstration of its activation. If a driver shifts into Drive while the charger is still attached, the infotainment system will display a notice requiring activation of the feature to override the lock. A warning also appears, stating that driving with the cable connected will cause damage to both the car and the Supercharger station, explicitly advising, &quot;Use only in an emergency.&quot; To engage the feature, drivers must hold the brake pedal and tap 'Activate' on the screen.</p><p>Access to this feature requires an upgrade to the latest software. It is currently functional only with Superchargers located in the US and is not supported on pre-2021 Model S and X vehicles. Tesla also stated on X that deliberate misuse &quot;will invoke additional penalties,&quot; and clarified that physical and monetary damages for such misuse could amount to &quot;up to $25k.&quot; It remains unclear whether Tesla intends to charge for activating the drive-away feature in genuine emergencies.</p><p>What to watch: Expansion of the feature to more regions and vehicle models.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Successfully captures the details of the feature and its context.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2275509/tesla-supercharger-emergency-drive-away-feature/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-10-05/tesla-rolls-out-emergency-drive-away-feature-after-superchar" data-title="Tesla Rolls Out Emergency Drive Away Feature After Supercharger Shooting"><b>Share:</b>
