       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-05 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Yuzvendra Chahal Retires from First-Class Cricket, Targets White-Ball Comeback</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Yuzvendra Chahal</span> <span>#First-Class</span> <span>#White-Ball</span></div>
       <p>Leg-spinner Yuzvendra Chahal announced his retirement from first-class cricket on Friday, October 2, 2026, via an Instagram post. This decision brings an end to a red-ball career that spanned 49 matches and yielded 148 wickets. Chahal's final first-class game was played for Northamptonshire in the County Championship Division Two against Middlesex at Lord's, a fixture that concluded on September 26, 2026.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-05/yuzvendra-chahal-retires-from-first-class-cricket-targets-wh.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>During his last appearance at Lord's, Chahal claimed two wickets, marking the conclusion of a three-year stint with Northamptonshire in English domestic red-ball cricket. Reflecting on his journey, Chahal stated, &quot;For me, red-ball cricket was never just another format. It was where I learned the meaning of patience, character, resilience and the pure joy of being out there for hours, fighting for every run and every wicket.&quot; He expressed that a Test cap was always a dream, one he carried for many years, but is genuinely content with his journey.</p><p>Chahal's first-class record reflects consistent wicket-taking ability, including six five-wicket hauls. His best figures in an innings stood at 6/44, demonstrating his capacity for impactful spells. Before his county engagements, he represented Haryana in domestic red-ball cricket in India. In his retirement statement, Chahal acknowledged the Haryana Cricket Association, Mr. Ranbir Singh Mahindra Sir, and Mr. Anirudh Singh Chaudhary Sir for their continuous support.</p><p>Crucially, Chahal clarified that he is not stepping away from white-ball cricket and intends to pursue opportunities in ODI and T20 formats. He emphatically stated, &quot;But I AM NOT DONE YET. The hunger to play for my country is still very much alive.&quot; He believes he still has cricket left in him and will give everything to earn another opportunity in limited-overs formats.</p><p>His last international appearance for India came in August 2023 against West Indies, and he has not represented the country at international level since then. Across 152 matches for India in ODIs and T20Is, Chahal has claimed 217 wickets, making him one of the more prolific white-ball spinners. At 36, Chahal, who was also part of India's 2024 T20 World Cup-winning squad, now shifts his entire focus to limited-overs cricket as he aims to add to his international record.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is comprehensive, well-structured, and strictly adheres to the provided source text.</p>
